{"timestamp": "2026-04-28T04:48:57.474798", "query": "baby 1 year 10 kg fever 101 F", "reply": "To determine the appropriate course of action for your 1-year-old baby who weighs 10 kg and has a fever of 101 F, I need to consider the clinical guidelines. \n\nSince the baby's fever is 101 F, which is considered mild to moderate, and there are no mentioned danger signs, this case can be classified as SAFE.\n\nFor a baby weighing 10 kg with a mild to moderate fever, the recommended dosage of paracetamol can be calculated as follows:\n- Paracetamol: 10 kg * 15 mg/kg = 150 mg\n\nIf using Crocin (120mg/5ml), the dose would be: 150 mg / 24 = 6.25 ml (approximately, every 6 hours)\nIf using Calpol (250mg/5ml), the dose would be: 150 mg / 50 = 3 ml (every 6 hours)\n\nPlease ensure not to exceed the maximum recommended doses in 24 hours. \n\nIt is essential to monitor the baby's temperature and overall condition closely. If the fever exceeds 102 F or if you notice any danger signs such as the baby being unconscious, not feeding at all, having blue lips, or experiencing a seizure, this would require urgent medical attention. \n\nAlso, note that ibuprofen can be considered for babies over 6 months old. For a 1-year-old, the ibuprofen dosage would be: 10 kg * 10 mg/kg = 100 mg\nIf using Ibugesic Plus (100mg/5ml), the dose would be: 100 mg / 20 = 5 ml (every 8 hours)", "mode": "data", "score": 0.741, "response_time_ms": 7561.11}
